--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHY OpenSSH portable, and the operator ruling (item vms-22a, parent vms-843)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Operator directive (2026-08-14): SSH is THE networking client that matters.
Do NOT hand-roll SSH — crypto and protocol are security-critical. PORT the real
OpenSSH (OpenBSD upstream + the portable tree, which has an OpenVMS-port lineage
via GNV). The original vms-843 body (a minimal from-scratch SSH-2 server) is
abandoned. Full plan: docs/design-openssh-port-ovmx.md.

This item (vms-22a) lands the CLIENT half first — ssh, then scp/sftp — because
the clients only CONNECT OUT and so ride the already-done BGn: client path
(vms-527); they are unblocked by the (still-pending) BGn: server path the sshd
swap (vms-0cd) needs.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHY 10.0p1 (the exact pin), and the p2 mirror note
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
10.0 is the current OpenSSH major (portable p-series). The pin is BY SHA256 —
that is the reproducibility invariant, not the file name. Note a mirror quirk
observed 2026-08-14: the OpenBSD CDN serves openssh-10.0p2.tar.gz with BYTES
IDENTICAL to openssh-10.0p1.tar.gz (same SHA256 021a2e70…, inner directory
openssh-10.0p1), and — the deciding detail — the SOURCE ITSELF self-identifies
as OpenSSH_10.0p2: the built binary prints "OpenSSH_10.0p2, LibreSSL 4.1.2" for
`ssh -V` (SSH_VERSION in version.h is p2). So the CONTENT is the security-fixed
p2 release; only the CDN's file name / inner-directory label reads p1. We pin
the exact tarball name that resolves reproducibly (openssh-10.0p1.tar.gz) with
its verified SHA256, and the acceptance test asserts the OpenSSH_10.0 banner so
the real version is checked, not assumed.

To move the pin (e.g. when the mirror publishes genuinely distinct p2 bytes, or
a later 10.x lands): update Pinned version / Tarball SHA256 / Inner directory /
URLs here AND the OSSH_VERSION / OSSH_SHA256 / OSSH_INNER defaults in
build-openssh.sh, then rerun third-party/openssh/test/run_ssh_build.sh in the
musl container to reconfirm the static build + the transport-shim compile-check.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
LAYOUT — fetch-vendored, NOT a source copy in-tree (same model as libcrypto)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Like third-party/libcrypto (and unlike third-party/tcc, a committed source
copy), OpenSSH is a large, actively-developed tree; committing it wholesale
would bloat the repo. This is a PINNED FETCH: build-openssh.sh fetches exactly
this tarball, verifies it against the SHA256 above (mismatch = hard fail), and
builds entirely out-of-tree (default /tmp) inside the project's containerized
musl build tooling (alpine:3.20). Nothing is installed on the host and no build
product ever lands in the repo (shared-host rule / "ALL deps containerized").

  build-openssh.sh          fetch (SHA256-verified) + build the ssh client
                            static against musl + the vendored libcrypto
                            (third-party/libcrypto). Optional OVMX transport
                            substitution (OVMX_BG_TRANSPORT=1, §below).
  ovmx/ovmx_bg_transport.h  OVMX port layer: route the ssh transport over the
  ovmx/ovmx_bg_transport.c  executive BGn: INET device (public $ASSIGN/$QIO),
                            NOT a raw socket(). Mirrors
                            src/vmstcpip/services/tcpip_client.h (vms-527).
  ovmx/sshconnect-bg.patch  the minimal, localized edit to the vendored
                            sshconnect.c (ssh_connect_direct) that calls the
                            shim, guarded by -DOVMX_BG_TRANSPORT.
  test/run_ssh_build.sh     acceptance: build static ssh; assert it is fully
                            static + prints its version; then compile-check the
                            transport shim against the real OVMX headers and the
                            patched sshconnect.c against the shim. CI job:
                            openssh-static-musl.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE OVMX TRANSPORT SUBSTITUTION (BGn:) — what is proven where
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Stock OpenSSH opens its TCP connection with socket()/connect() in sshconnect.c
and then drives KEX/auth/channel I/O as read()/write()/poll() on that fd. On
OVMX a VMS program reaches TCP the ordinary VMS way: $ASSIGN TCPIP$DEVICE: +
$QIO into the executive-resident BGn: socket (vms.ko, over the host kernel's
in-kernel socket API) — there is NO userspace socket stack. ovmx_bg_connect()
substitutes that path: it $ASSIGNs/$QIOs a BGn: connection and hands OpenSSH an
ordinary pollable socketpair fd that pump threads bridge to the VMS channel, so
the vendored packet layer is unchanged (design §2.3, the "BSD-socket veneer over
BGn:"). If /dev/vms is absent, $ASSIGN returns SS$_NOSUCHDEV and the connect
fails honestly (errno ENODEV) — never a raw-socket fallback (Rule 9 / INV-6).

Proven by CI (openssh-static-musl, this item):
  1. OpenSSH 10.0p1 ssh cross-builds FULLY STATIC against musl + the vendored
     LibreSSL libcrypto, zero source patches (--without-zlib --without-pam).
  2. ovmx_bg_transport.c compiles clean (-Wall -Wextra) against the real OVMX
     system-service headers (src/libvms/include).
  3. The sshconnect-bg.patch applies to the pinned source and the patched TU
     compiles with -DOVMX_BG_TRANSPORT, its object referencing ovmx_bg_connect
     (the substitution is wired, not just present on disk).

Deferred to the QEMU Kernel-Executive proof (continuation, see the PR/design):
  building ssh as the OVMX IMGACT image linked against libvms and proving a REAL
  SSH key exchange + auth + remote command over BGn: against an in-guest loopback
  sshd on a real /dev/vms (Rule 9), honest-skip (77) without the executive.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Rule 8 / clean-room
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
OpenSSH (BSD/ISC) and LibreSSL (ISC/OpenSSL-style) are permissive OSS we may
legitimately read, port and adapt — Rule 8's HARD INVARIANT is about
reverse-engineered VMS formats/protocols and VSI/HPE source/binaries, which we
never touch. The VMS-facing surface the shim speaks (TCPIP$DEVICE:, the QIO
function map, the 8-byte BGn: sockaddr) is from public VSI OpenVMS TCP/IP
Services docs + the already-landed BGn: driver, and the shim is labeled an OVMX
design choice — no VMS-authentic claim is made for it.
